 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np14-01",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Rhetorical questions",
   "quote": "quid loquor? aut ubi sum? quae mentem insania mutat?",
   "trans": "what am I saying? where am I? what madness changes my mind?",
   "line": "595",
   "body": "Three short questions in a row show Dido coming to her senses with a jolt, aware that her orders are useless.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-03",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Apostrophe",
   "quote": "infelix Dido",
   "trans": "unhappy Dido",
   "line": "596",
   "body": "Dido speaks to herself in the third person, as if she were watching her own tragedy from outside. Infelix is the word most often used of her in the poem.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-04",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Word choice",
   "quote": "facta impia",
   "trans": "impious deeds",
   "line": "596",
   "body": "Impia is pointed: Aeneas is famous for pietas, and Dido now calls his conduct its opposite. (The phrase could also refer to her own deeds, the broken vow to Sychaeus, which makes it more painful still.)",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-05",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Tense",
   "quote": "tum decuit, cum sceptra dabas",
   "trans": "then was the time, when you were handing over your sceptre",
   "line": "597",
   "body": "The past tenses look back to her mistake: she should have been wary when she shared her kingdom with him, not now.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-06",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Exclamation",
   "quote": "en dextra fidesque",
   "trans": "so much for his right hand and his good faith",
   "line": "597",
   "body": "Sarcastic en: the right hand was the pledge of a promise, and Dido mocks the trust she placed in it.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-07",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Anaphora",
   "quote": "quem secum … quem subiisse",
   "trans": "the man who … the man who",
   "line": "598–599",
   "body": "The repeated quem lists the famous proofs of Aeneas' piety, carrying the household gods and his father, only to mock them.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-08",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "aiunt",
   "trans": "they say",
   "line": "598",
   "body": "One word turns praise into hearsay: Dido now doubts the stories she once found so moving.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-09",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Rhetorical question",
   "quote": "non potui abreptum divellere corpus et undis spargere?",
   "trans": "could I not have seized his body, torn it apart and scattered it on the waves?",
   "line": "600–601",
   "body": "The violent verbs build a picture of savage revenge, which shows how far grief has turned to rage.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-10",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Enjambment",
   "quote": "et undis spargere",
   "trans": "and scatter it on the waves",
   "line": "600–601",
   "body": "Spargere spills over to the next line, like the scattered body it describes.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-11",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Anaphora",
   "quote": "non socios, non ipsum",
   "trans": "not his comrades, not himself",
   "line": "600–601",
   "body": "The repeated non piles up her missed chances, each victim closer to Aeneas.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-12",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Vivid detail",
   "quote": "patriisque epulandum ponere mensis",
   "trans": "and set him to be eaten at his father's table",
   "line": "602",
   "body": "The gerundive conjures the most horrifying revenge of myth, a son served to his father, which shows Dido's thoughts at their darkest.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-13",
   "cat": "order",
   "label": "Emphatic position",
   "quote": "Ascanium",
   "trans": "Ascanius",
   "line": "602",
   "body": "The boy's name is held back to the start of line 602 and stands out, so the reader feels the shock of her imagined target.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-14",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Conditional",
   "quote": "faces in castra tulissem implessemque foros flammis",
   "trans": "I would have carried torches into the camp and filled the decks with flames",
   "line": "604–605",
   "body": "The past unreal subjunctives describe what she could have done, and the fire imagery anticipates her own pyre.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-15",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Polysyndeton",
   "quote": "natumque patremque cum genere",
   "trans": "son and father and the whole race",
   "line": "605–606",
   "body": "Her imagined destruction sweeps up every generation, a foretaste of the curse on Aeneas' descendants.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-16",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "memet super ipsa dedissem",
   "trans": "I would have thrown myself on top",
   "line": "606",
   "body": "The doubled pronoun, memet ... ipsa, insists that she would have died with them, so murder and suicide merge.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-17",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Tricolon",
   "quote": "Sol … Iuno … Hecate",
   "trans": "Sun … Juno … Hecate",
   "line": "607–609",
   "body": "Dido calls on three great powers in turn, each in a longer phrase, so her prayer grows in solemnity.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-18",
   "cat": "sound",
   "label": "Onomatopoeia",
   "quote": "ululata",
   "trans": "howled to",
   "line": "609",
   "body": "The word imitates the cries of the worshippers at night, which gives the invocation of Hecate an eerie sound.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-19",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Imperatives",
   "quote": "accipite … advertite … audite",
   "trans": "receive … turn … hear",
   "line": "611–612",
   "body": "Three commands to the gods close the section, so Dido speaks with the confidence of someone owed justice.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-20",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Ascanius",
   "quote": "Ascanium",
   "trans": "Ascanius",
   "line": "602",
   "body": "Aeneas' son, also called Iulus, ancestor of the Julian family to which Julius Caesar and Augustus belonged.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-21",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Hecate",
   "quote": "Hecate",
   "trans": "Hecate",
   "line": "609",
   "body": "Goddess of crossroads, magic and the underworld, worshipped at night at places where three roads met.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-22",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "The Dirae",
   "quote": "Dirae ultrices",
   "trans": "the avenging Furies",
   "line": "610",
   "body": "Goddesses of vengeance, who punish wrongs such as broken oaths and crimes against family.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np14-23",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Elissa",
   "quote": "Elissae",
   "trans": "of Elissa",
   "line": "610",
   "body": "Dido's other name, perhaps her Phoenician name. Here she speaks of herself as dying, morientis.",
   "exam": ""
  }
 ],
 "section": 14,
 "questions": [
  "Dido moves from confusion (line 595) to fantasies of revenge (lines 600–606) to prayer (lines 607–612). How does Virgil mark each change of mood in the Latin?",
  "Dido mocks the reports of Aeneas' piety in lines 597–599. Is her scorn justified? How does this passage test the idea of Aeneas as pius?",
  "The revenge Dido imagines in lines 600–602 recalls monstrous myths such as Atreus and Thyestes. Why might Virgil give her such horrific thoughts at this point?"
 ],
 "context": [
  {
   "h": "Dido's gods",
   "body": "<p>Dido ends this section with a formal prayer, calling on the powers who see and punish wrongdoing. Sol, the Sun, sees everything on earth; Juno, goddess of marriage, presided over her \"wedding\" in the cave and is Carthage's protector; Hecate, goddess of crossroads, magic and the underworld, is linked with the rites Dido has been preparing; the Dirae, or Furies, avenge wrongs, especially broken oaths and crimes against family.</p><p>The prayer is the first half of a curse. Its second half, in the next section, asks these gods for specific punishments on Aeneas and on his descendants.</p>"
  }
 ]
}